## ============================================================================
## Logging / Telemetry
## ============================================================================
#
# HYPERDX_LOG_LEVEL=info
# USAGE_STATS_ENABLED=false

## ============================================================================
## ClickHouse Sources
## ============================================================================
## Compose seeds these from config/hyperdx/default-sources.json, which the
## service mounts and passes as DEFAULT_SOURCES_FILE. Set the JSON inline here to
## override that file for this deployment.
#
# DEFAULT_SOURCES=

## ============================================================================
## Auth
## ============================================================================
## Compose runs `oidc-proxy`, the same posture as Kubernetes: HyperDX verifies the
## engine's ES384 token against http://dfe-engine:8000/.well-known/jwks.json, and
## a caller who carries none is unauthenticated. The console mirrors its session
## into a dfe_token cookie, so signing in to the DFE UI signs you in here.
##
## docker-compose.yml sets DFE_AUTH_MODE, and an `environment:` entry beats this
## file -- so the switch back to `header-dev` for a HyperDX running without an
## engine is DFE_HYPERDX_AUTH_MODE=header-dev in .env. That mode signs in whoever
## X-Forwarded-Email and X-Forwarded-Groups name. The `auth` profile's oauth2-proxy
## sets both after its group check. Without it, dfe-hyperdx-proxy and dfe-proxy
## strip them, and X-OIDC-Subject and -Groups, from every caller, so HyperDX falls
## back to its own login. Any other header named below is one a caller can send.
#
# DFE_AUTH_HEADER_EMAIL=x-forwarded-email
# DFE_AUTH_HEADER_GROUPS=x-forwarded-groups
# HYPERDX_API_KEY=